*Usability Testing*

==== [Lecture Topic Task]: Observation Based Evaluation of User Hesitation During Appointment Scheduling Goal
:author: Victoria Miranda Osuna

===== Goal

Evaluate how user hesitation and uncertainty affect the appointment scheduling workflow by analyzing observable interaction behaviors during usability testing sessions.

===== Proposed Solution

    - Define observable hesitation indicators during scheduling tasks.

        * Monitor pauses before selections are made.
        * Observe repeated clicking or changing selections.
        * Identify moments where users stop to reread interface information.

    - Analyze how users respond when selecting appointment types, dates, and time slots.

        * Evaluate whether users understand elements they can select.
        * Observe how easily users navigate available dates.
        * Monitor whether users recognize available versus unavailable slots.
        * Identify sections that require repeated attempts.

    - Evaluate whether workflow transitions create confusion or interruptions.

        * Analyze whether users maintain confidence throughout scheduling.
        * Identify opportunities to improve clarity and navigation flow.

    - Use usability observation techniques to identify points where users pause, backtrack, or consult interface guidance based on the previously mentioned points we can infer the cause of these actions.

===== Success Criteria

    * Observable hesitation behaviors are clearly identified.
    * User interaction patterns are analyzed using usability testing principles.
    * Workflow interruptions and uncertainty points are documented.
    * Recommendations improve workflow clarity and reduce user hesitation.
